course: dictionary
lesson: 13718
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words can be a thing and also an action. Institute is one of them. As a noun it names an organisation set up for a special purpose. As a verb it means to start or set something up, such as a rule. The context tells you which one you have.
explain: «The scientists work at a research institute near the university.» Here institute is a place where people do research, which means careful study to find out new things. The words scientists and research are your clues.
explain: «The king decided to institute a new rule about school hours.» Now institute is a verb. The king did not build a place. He started a new rule. Try swapping in set up: the sentence still makes sense, so you know you have the right meaning.
fact: An institute is an organisation set up for a special purpose, such as research, teaching or science.
fact: Institute can also mean the building that the organisation uses.
fact: As a verb, «to institute» something means to start or set it up, like a rule or a system.
fact: Institute is a formal word, so you will often meet it in news, history and school subjects.
fact: Phrases you may see include «research institute», «institute of technology» and «institute a rule».
exercise: «The new research institute opened last spring.» What is an institute here? | A type of boat | A long walk | *A place set up for research | A kind of sweet
exercise: «The council will institute a new recycling system next year.» What does institute mean here? | To paint the walls | To break or destroy | To visit quietly | *To start or set up
exercise: «The institute runs science classes for young students.» Which word tells you the institute is a place of learning? | runs | the | young | *classes
test: «The university built a new institute for medical research.» Is institute a place or an action here? | An action, meaning to start | *A place | Both at the same time | Neither, it is a colour
test: «Parliament decided to institute a law about litter.» What does institute mean in this sentence? | To drop or throw away | To eat quickly | To measure carefully | *To start or set up
exam: «The institute was set up to study the stars, and later it began to institute new courses.» What does institute mean in the second part? | *To start or set up | A kind of research | A building for the stars | To close down
